[38;5;252m[1m## Two things worth knowing[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I validated that the suite can actually detect bugs rather than trusting green output. Twelve mutations to the Rust lib (all reverted) each produced the expected failures — dropping a [38;5;10mFLAG_DELETE[0m term failed 8 tests, an [38;5;10madd_element[0m off-by-one failed 12, removing [38;5;10mfree_array[0m's NULL guard SIGSEGVs. The one mutation with no effect is [38;5;10mmatrixsum[0m's [38;5;10m-1[0m sentinel, which is genuinely unreachable because [38;5;10minit_array(2)[0m cannot fail on a healthy heap. Table is in [38;5;10mERRORS.md[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two C paths leave [38;5;10marr->data[0m dangling and are untestable as differentials rather than as divergences: when [38;5;10mexpand_array[0m fails via glibc's [38;5;10mrealloc(p, 0)[0m (reachable with [38;5;10mcapacity == 0[0m), the C does not clear or roll back [38;5;10mdata[0m, so a subsequent [38;5;10madd_element[0m or [38;5;10mfree_array[0m is a double free in the original C too. The tests assert the return code and the untouched [38;5;10msize[0m/[38;5;10mcapacity[0m, then deliberately leak the 24-byte struct instead of provoking UB. This is documented in [38;5;10mERRORS.md[0m rows E5 and E7.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One correction to my own work: my first draft asserted the factory matrix checksum was 868. The real value is 916 (10 + 160 + 746) — both libraries agreed all along; my arithmetic was wrong. Fixed in the test and in [38;5;10mCONFIGS.md[0m. Nothing in [38;5;10mc_src/[0m was modified.[0m[0m
